# The Envelope of Seeds on the Windowsill

Fanny Sturtevant sat down at the maple kitchen table at eight-fifty-two, opened the ninth cigar box, and set the kettle for tea.

Frances Louisa "Fanny" Bickford Sturtevant — Craftsbury Elementary '54, Craftsbury Academy '58, UVM College of Agriculture and Life Sciences '66 (Plant and Soil Science, high honors, senior project on the winter-hardiness of dry beans in the Northeast Kingdom under Professor Wendell Lockwood in the spring), a county agricultural extension agent for Orleans and Essex counties from the September of 1969 until her retirement on the last Friday of June, 2010 — has lived in the small white farmhouse two miles up the West Hill Road, four hundred yards past the sugarhouse where her father-in-law Everett boiled sap from 1948 until the spring of 2001, since a warm Saturday afternoon in the October of 1968, when she and her husband Alden Bickford Sturtevant — UVM '65, dairy farmer at his father's place off Ketchum Hill until the herd was sold in the March of 2004, town lister from 1981 to 2007 — had signed the deed at the Craftsbury Town Clerk's office with the ink from Alden's father's Parker "51" and driven the six miles home in the Ford F-100 that Alden's cousin at Farr Motors in Hardwick had sold them for eight hundred dollars in the June.

Alden passed at home, in the small back sitting room with the west window that looks out on the two Cortland apple trees his father set out in 1946, on the seventh of April, 2018, of the pancreatic cancer Dr. Jonathan Grady at Copley Hospital had told them, in the January, was not going to see the mud season through. Marcia Dubois, RN, from Caledonia Home Health & Hospice, had been in the room. Fanny had been in the room. The old brindle border collie Bess, thirteen, had been on the hooked rug.

The kitchen garden — the reason there is no lawn on the south side of the house — is a hundred and ten feet by seventy, walled on the north and east in stone Alden's grandfather set in 1912, worked in Fanny's own quiet three-year rotation since a Saturday in the April of 1970, and, on the sideboard against the west wall of the summer kitchen, catalogued in twelve wooden Antonio y Cleopatra cigar boxes Alden brought home from the Craftsbury General Store one at a time between the June of 1971 and the November of 1983.

Inside the twelve boxes — arranged by family, the way any extension agent would arrange them, and the way no seed vault will ever be asked to replicate — are one thousand three hundred and eighty-four small brown-paper coin envelopes.

She has been labeling them, at the maple kitchen table, since a Saturday in the April of 1970.

## What the Envelope Is

The envelope is a plain number-one brown-kraft coin envelope, two and a quarter by three and a half, from the small stationer's on Main Street in Hardwick that has sold coin envelopes to the Caledonia County town clerks since the Vermont Constitutional Convention. Alden bought Fanny the first box of a thousand, for their second anniversary, in the July of 1970. She has re-ordered every fourth June since.

The label reads, in the round agricultural-extension pencil hand Fanny learned from her boss Merton Perry at the Orleans County Extension Office in the September of 1969, and in the small careful three-line grammar Professor Lockwood taught the Plant and Soil Science seniors to use on their winter-fair displays in the spring of 1966:

*Variety. Grower. Year and place of the saving. One line, if there is a story.*

On the twenty-eight envelopes on the kitchen table at nine-eleven this morning, the one-line stories read, in Fanny's own pencil:

*Vermont Cranberry Bean, ninth generation, from Ida Louise Bickford's coat pocket, October 1898.*

*Gilfeather Turnip, seed stock from John Gilfeather's own Wardsboro field, given to me by his niece Ellen at the Tunbridge World's Fair, September 1978.*

*Roy's Calais Flint Corn, seed from Roy Fisk's north field in East Calais, saved before the last frost of 2003, the September after the barn burned.*

*True Red Cranberry Bean, thought extinct 1948, rediscovered by John Withee in Lynn MA 1981, gifted to me by John himself at the Common Ground Fair in the September of 1983.*

*Craftsbury Common Buff Belle Onion, a landrace saved by Fanny Bickford Sturtevant on the West Hill Road since 1974, not offered by any commercial house.*

Twelve boxes. One thousand three hundred and eighty-four envelopes. Two hundred and eleven named varieties. Seventy-four of them are, in the plain judgment of the Seed Savers Exchange listbook in Decorah, Iowa, no longer grown by any other Yearbook member north of the Mason–Dixon line.

She has, in the small green cloth-bound notebook on the sideboard beneath the sixth cigar box — a notebook she bought at Farrow's stationery in Barton the week after Alden's funeral — decided, envelope by envelope, over eight winters and seven summers, where every seed line in the house is going to go.

## What the Sideboard Says

The sideboard says, in the plain Extension-office pencil of a woman who has been walking bean rows in Orleans County since Nixon:

The heritage dry beans — the Vermont Cranberry, the True Red Cranberry, the Yellow Eye, the Marfax, the Black Turtle Alden grew in the front field the summer their daughter Emily was born — are going to the UVM Wolcott Research Farm on the Randolph Center road, where Professor Wendell Lockwood's grandnephew Alec, class of 2018, is now the assistant director of the Legacy Seed Trial and where the freezer in the north cold-room will hold Fanny's beans at minus-twenty in a small labeled acid-free box until a summer nobody has planted yet.

The flint corns — Roy's Calais, Byron's Dutton, the Abenaki flour corn Chief Homer St. Francis's cousin Bea gave her at the Highgate powwow in the August of 1994 — are going to the Abenaki Land Link project at the Nulhegan Band office in Brownington, where Bea's granddaughter Ashley is the seed keeper and where the corns will be planted, one row at a time, in the small demonstration plot behind the Nulhegan Longhouse next June.

The three-hundred-and-eighty envelopes of squash, cucumber, gourd, and melon — the Kakai pumpkin, the Boston Marrow, the Hidatsa Shield Figure, the Buttercup from Dr. Rennie Larson's own 1932 seed at UVM — are going to the Sterling College Farm in Craftsbury Common, three miles down the West Hill Road, where Fanny herself was on the Farm Advisory Board from the January of 2011 until the pandemic in the March of 2020, and where the Sustainable Food Systems seniors will grow them out, one variety a year for twenty-eight years, in the small teaching plot behind Simpson Hall.

The alliums, the greens, the culinary herbs, and the small red envelope of medicinal calendula seed the Craftsbury midwife Nancy Cross gave Fanny for their granddaughter's birth in the July of 1999 — six hundred and forty envelopes — are going to the Craftsbury Public Library seed library, twenty-two hundred feet down the West Hill Road, where the librarian Emily Woodard has been keeping a small oak card-catalog drawer of donated seed for the town gardeners since a Wednesday morning in the March of 2016.

The old Sturtevant kitchen-garden onions, the herbs, and the two shelves of tomato seed Alden hand-selected off the vine on the south wall from 1974 until the summer he could not walk the row — three hundred and eleven envelopes — are staying on the West Hill Road, in the twelfth cigar box on the sideboard, for their daughter Emily, DVM Cornell '97, of the small dairy on the Wolcott Road, and for Emily's daughter Hannah, a sophomore at Sterling College in the fall, who has been thinning the beet row on Saturday mornings with her grandmother since a June in 2011.

Every one of those decisions is written down in the small green cloth-bound notebook on the sideboard.

Every one of those envelopes is a small quiet handshake from Fanny — at the kitchen table, on a Monday morning in July — to the field or the freezer or the card-catalog drawer where the seed is going to spend the winter it is not going to spend in her house.

The envelope is the announcement.

## What the Burlington Second Floor Thinks It Knows

On the second floor of a converted brick building on the north end of a green in Burlington, Vermont, on the same Monday morning at nine-oh-seven, the associate director of leadership annual giving at the University of Vermont Foundation — a warm and capable woman named Callie Rousseau Whitman, class of 2011, hired from a regional health-system foundation in Rutland in the fall of 2023 — is looking at the July report on the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences segment on the laptop at a standing desk under a window that faces Waterman Green.

The file open on Callie's second monitor, in the small quiet suggestion column of the fall appeal queue, reads:

*STURTEVANT, FRANCES L. (née BICKFORD). CALS '66. West Hill Rd., Craftsbury VT 05827. Segment 4. Annual fund lapsed since 1994. Last gift $25. Spouse deceased 04/2018. Wealth-screen capacity range $0–$25K. No engagement noted. Not on any current volunteer roster. Suggested ask, fall appeal: $35.*

The wealth-screen vendor ran Fanny in the March of 2025 and returned, courteously, the same $0–$25K capacity band on the strength of the same public real-estate record — one white farmhouse two miles up the West Hill Road, assessed in 2024 at two hundred and twenty-four thousand dollars, paid off in 2003. The screen did not know about the boxes. The screen did not know about the envelopes. The screen did not know about the notebook.

Callie has never met Fanny Sturtevant.

Callie has never opened the ninth cigar box.

Callie has never driven up the West Hill Road on a warm Monday morning in July.

Neither has anybody on the second floor of the converted brick building on the north end of Waterman Green, since a Wednesday in the September of 1994.

## What the Envelope Knows

The envelope is the plainest legacy document a donor will ever write.

It is labeled, in pencil, in the round Extension-office hand of a woman who has been walking bean rows in Orleans County since the September of 1969. It is not on a wealth screen. It does not appear on any legacy-society roster. It is not on the reply card at the bottom of the fall appeal. It is not, in any measurable sense, in the CRM.

It is on the flap of a two-and-a-quarter-by-three-and-a-half brown-kraft coin envelope, in the ninth of twelve cigar boxes on the pine sideboard against the west wall of a summer kitchen two miles up the West Hill Road in Craftsbury Common. That is the whole document.

Each of Fanny's envelopes, pencilled between the April of 1970 and a warm Monday morning in the last week of July, 2026, is a small quiet piece of philanthropic writing. Each is a decision. Each names — not in dollars, but in generations, in row-feet, in cold winters survived — the small careful institution that Fanny, at the kitchen table on a Monday morning, has decided will inherit that piece of her attention.

If a screening vendor produced, in an email to Callie Rousseau Whitman at eight fifty-seven on Monday morning, the plain sentence *your donor Frances Bickford Sturtevant has, over the past eight years, personally hand-labeled three hundred and eighty small brown coin envelopes of dry-bean seed that she intends to bequeath to the UVM Wolcott Research Farm, and there are ninety-two more to go*, Callie would close her laptop, cancel her ten-thirty, and get in her Forester.

The envelopes are on the West Hill Road.

The Forester is in the Waterman lot.

Both women are, at nine-oh-seven on a warm Monday morning in July, thinking about the same freezer.

Neither one knows the other is.

## A small honest note about Rōmy

Rōmy did not save Fanny's seed. Fanny did, in the ninth of twelve cigar boxes on the pine sideboard, on the twenty-sixth of September in the last long autumn, in the round Extension-office hand she has used to write soil-test notes since the September of 1969.

Rōmy did not label the envelope. Fanny did, at the maple kitchen table, at nine-eleven on Monday morning, in the plain unhurried grammar Professor Wendell Lockwood taught the Plant and Soil Science seniors to use in the spring of 1966.

What Rōmy can do is notice.

Not instead of Callie Whitman. Callie is the reason the story ends the way it should. She is the one who will drive to Craftsbury Common, in the third week of September, park the Forester at the Sterling College farm gate, walk the last two miles up the West Hill Road with a small tin of gingersnaps and a thermos of tea, sit on the porch with Fanny for the whole afternoon, ask about Alden and about the two Cortland apples, admire the stone-walled garden without asking a single question about it, and — before she goes — thank Fanny, quietly, for the sixty years.

Rōmy is the small note on Callie's Wednesday-morning list, six weeks before she gets in the Forester, that reads: *Frances Bickford Sturtevant ('66 CALS, Craftsbury VT). A named seed line — Sturtevant Family Cranberry Bean, ninth generation — has been listed in the Craftsbury Public Library's seed-library card catalog since a Wednesday in March 2016, and a small envelope of the same variety was gifted to the Nulhegan Abenaki Land Link seed keeper at the Highgate powwow in August 2024. Suggested: a plain notecard, this week, from the director of the UVM Wolcott Research Farm, in her own hand, thanking Fanny for the sixty years and asking whether she might, sometime this fall, walk the Farm's cold-room with her and Alec. Do not put a case for support in the envelope. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

The notecard goes out on Monday.

Fanny reads it, at the maple kitchen table, on Thursday afternoon.

By the second week of October, on a warm Saturday morning, Callie is in the summer kitchen on the West Hill Road, on the ladder-back chair by the west window, with a cup of Red Rose tea and a small plate of the maple sugar cookies Emily brought down from the Wolcott Road, and the ninth cigar box open on the maple kitchen table so Callie can read, in pencil, the label on the twenty-eighth envelope.

The reading is Callie's. The notice is the tool's. The envelope is Fanny's.

## A small assignment, with love ♡

This month, or on the first warm Saturday morning after the peas come in, drive out into your service area — up the West Hill Road, or down the county highway, or two towns over to the small yellow farmhouse where the retired 4-H leader is putting up strawberry jam — and do one small unfashionable thing.

Do not look at the framed diplomas in the parlor. Everybody looks at the diplomas. Do not look at the photographs on the mantel. Everybody looks at the photographs.

Look at the sideboard.

Walk over to the sideboard, or the pine cupboard, or the small painted shelf above the summer-kitchen sink. Find the wooden box, or the tin, or the small oak card-catalog drawer.

Open it.

Look for a small brown envelope. Kraft paper. Two and a quarter by three and a half. A pencil label in a round unhurried hand. A variety. A grower. A year and a place of the saving.

If there is one, read it out loud, quietly, to yourself, the way Fanny Sturtevant read her grandmother Ida Louise's own pencil hand out loud to Alden in the summer kitchen on Sunday afternoons in the July of 1970. Read the variety. Read the year. Read the last small line — the one about the coat pocket in October 1898, or the one about the September the barn burned, or the one about the Highgate powwow, or the one about the granddaughter thinning the beet row on a Saturday morning in June.

Then, before you leave the house, ask your donor, quietly, one small question.

*Are you working on the boxes yet?*

She is.

She has been, on Monday mornings at the maple kitchen table, since a Saturday in the April of 1970.

She has not, until you asked her, been asked. ♡
